======================================================================================================================
A.  REPRODUCTION: the number itself is not in dispute
======================================================================================================================
  canonical a0 = 9.36e-11: Spearman rho = +0.6667, Pearson r = +0.6666, EXACT permutation p = 0.0831 (1.73 sigma)
  alt       a0 = 1.13e-10: Spearman rho = +0.6667, Pearson r = +0.6681, EXACT permutation p = 0.0831 (1.73 sigma)
  [PASS] V0 (REPRODUCTION) g03's headline rho = +0.667 at p = 0.082 must reproduce independently, on both footings, with the p-value computed EXACTLY over all 40320 orderings rather than by Monte Carlo   (rho = +0.6667 / +0.6667, exact p = 0.0831 / 0.0831 against g03's 50000-permutation 0.0820 / 0.0822. The arithmetic is correct. Everything below is about the INFERENCE, not the number)

======================================================================================================================
B.  WHAT THE RESIDUAL ACTUALLY IS
======================================================================================================================
  system           branch  log M*  sigma    resid  rank(resid)  rank(-logM*)  rank(beta)
  Draco          isolated    5.46    9.1   +0.617            6             6           6
  Sculptor       isolated    6.36    9.2   +0.177            3             2           3
  Fornax              EFE    7.63   11.7   -0.380            0             0           4
  Carina         isolated    5.58    6.6   +0.279            4             5           5
  Sextans        isolated    5.64    7.9   +0.404            5             4           2
  Leo I               EFE    6.74    9.2   -0.251            1             1           0
  Leo II         isolated    5.87    6.6   +0.134            2             3           1
  Ursa Minor     isolated    5.46    9.5   +0.654            7             7           7

  Spearman(resid, -log M*)  = +0.9524   <-- the residual is a stellar-mass rank order
  Spearman(-log M*, beta)   = +0.6905   <-- and THIS is the claimed correlation, without gravity
  Spearman(resid, beta)     = +0.6667
  Kendall tau(-log M*, beta) = +0.536  -- the whole test, restated: Hayashi's beta_z anticorrelates with M*
  [FAIL] V1 (THE RESIDUAL IS A MASS ORDERING) for the correlation to be about orbits, the residual must carry rank information that stellar mass does not already carry. Spearman(resid, -log M*) must be below 0.9   (Spearman(resid, -log M*) = +0.9524: seven of the eight objects are in the same rank position in the residual as in inverse stellar mass. And Spearman(-log M*, beta) = +0.6905 is LARGER than the claimed +0.6667, so stellar mass alone outperforms the framework's residual)

======================================================================================================================
C.  THE ALGEBRA: R_half cancels, and the residual is 2 log(sigma) - 0.5 log(M*)
======================================================================================================================
  isolated branch: g_pred = sqrt(G M a0)/R and g_obs = 3 sigma^2/R, so R_half cancels IDENTICALLY:
      Draco         resid = +0.6169   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = +0.6169   EXACT
      Sculptor      resid = +0.1767   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = +0.1767   EXACT
      Fornax        resid = -0.3803   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = -0.2504   (EFE branch, differs)
      Carina        resid = +0.2792   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = +0.2792   EXACT
      Sextans       resid = +0.4035   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = +0.4035   EXACT
      Leo I         resid = -0.2511   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = -0.0126   (EFE branch, differs)
      Leo II        resid = +0.1344   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = +0.1344   EXACT
      Ursa Minor    resid = +0.6542   log10(3 sigma^2/sqrt(G M a0)) = +0.6542   EXACT
  spread of 2*log10(sigma) = 0.497 dex; of 0.5*log10(M*) = 1.086 dex -- ratio 2.18
  the two EFE-branch objects are ['Fornax', 'Leo I'], which are the two most massive, so the branch choice is itself a stellar-mass indicator
  [FAIL] V2 (VARIANCE BUDGET) for the residual to be a dynamical statistic, the kinematic term 2 log(sigma) must contribute at least as much spread as the photometric term 0.5 log(M*)   (2*log10(sigma) spans 0.497 dex, 0.5*log10(M*) spans 1.086 dex. On the six isolated-branch objects the residual is EXACTLY the deep-MOND Faber-Jackson residual and is dominated 2.2:1 by stellar mass by construction)

======================================================================================================================
D.  MUTATION CONTROLS: which input carries the correlation?
======================================================================================================================
  M-dispersions frozen to the sample mean (NO kinematics)        rho = +0.6905 (canonical), +0.6905 (alt)
  M-stellar masses frozen to the sample mean (kinematics kept)   rho = +0.2619 (canonical), +0.2619 (alt)
  M-baseline, nothing frozen                                       rho = +0.6667 (canonical), +0.6667 (alt)
  [FAIL] V3 (THE MUTATION THAT DECIDES IT) a trajectory-dependent modification expresses itself through the KINEMATICS. So deleting the kinematics -- setting every velocity dispersion to the sample mean and leaving only M*, R_half and Galactocentric distance -- must DESTROY the correlation with anisotropy   (with every dispersion frozen the correlation is rho = +0.6905, i.e. LARGER than the baseline +0.6667. Freezing the stellar masses instead and keeping the kinematics collapses it to +0.2619. The correlation lives entirely in the stellar masses; the dynamics can be removed without loss. Whatever this measures, it is not a property of the orbits)

======================================================================================================================
E.  g03's CONFOUND CHECK A5 IS COMPUTED IN THE WRONG CURRENCY
======================================================================================================================
  PEARSON partial(resid, beta | log M*) -- what g03 reports as A5: +0.7172 (canonical), +0.7193 (alt), exact p = 0.0757
  SPEARMAN partial, same variables on ranks -- matching the headline statistic: +0.1721 (canonical), +0.1721 (alt), exact p = 0.7017 (0.38 sigma)
  g03's headline statistic is Spearman rho and its own stated weakest link is that 'the rank ordering is what
  the statistic uses'. Its partial() is nevertheless a Pearson partial on the raw variables. The two disagree
  by more than the null sd, so the sign of A5's verdict is a choice of statistic, at N = 8.
  [FAIL] V4 (CURRENCY) the confound check must be computed in the same currency as the headline statistic. A Spearman headline requires a Spearman partial. The two must agree to within the null sd   (Pearson partial +0.7172 against Spearman partial +0.1721, a difference of 0.545 against a null sd of 0.378. In the claim's OWN currency, controlling for stellar mass takes the correlation to +0.1721 at exact p = 0.702 = 0.38 sigma -- consistent with zero. g03's verdict line 'Controlling for stellar mass does NOT weaken it (+0.717)' does not survive this)
  
  Local Volume Database compilation, same three statistics: Spearman(resid, beta) = +0.5476, Spearman(resid, -log M*) = +0.8095, Spearman partial | log M* = +0.4503
  
  AGAINST INTEREST -- the same two mutations on the Local Volume Database compilation are NOT as clean:
      baseline +0.5476;  dispersions frozen +0.4048;  stellar masses frozen +0.4286
      On that compilation the kinematics carry about as much as the masses, so the 'photometry carries all of
      it' statement is specific to f09's hand-entered table. What is compilation-INDEPENDENT is the near-
      degeneracy of the residual with stellar mass and the fact that the rank partial is consistent with zero.
  [PASS] V5 (INDEPENDENT COMPILATION) the same diagnosis must hold on the Local Volume Database numbers, otherwise it is an artefact of f09's hand-entered table. The rank-partial on that compilation must also be consistent with zero for the diagnosis to stand   (Local Volume Database: raw rho = +0.5476, resid-vs-mass rank correlation +0.8095, Spearman partial controlling stellar mass = +0.4503. Same diagnosis, independent structural data. NOTE this check PASSING is a point AGAINST the claim, not for it)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  THE NUMBER REPRODUCES AND THE INFERENCE DOES NOT. rho = +0.667, exact p = 0.0831, 1.73 sigma,
  identical on both footings. But in rank terms the residual IS the stellar-mass ordering
  (Spearman = +0.952), and stellar mass alone correlates with Hayashi's beta_z at
  +0.690 -- BETTER than the framework's residual does. Deleting every velocity dispersion from the
  residual leaves the correlation at +0.690; deleting the stellar masses instead collapses it to +0.262.
  The correlation is carried by the photometry, not the dynamics, so it cannot be a fingerprint of a
  modification that attaches to trajectories.

  AND g03's OWN CONFOUND CHECK REVERSES. A5 reports a Pearson partial (+0.717) while the headline is Spearman;
  the matching Spearman partial is +0.172 at exact p = 0.70. 'Controlling for stellar mass does NOT
  weaken it' is a currency mismatch, and the verdict line built on it should be withdrawn.

  ORDINARY COLD DARK MATTER PRODUCES THIS SIGNATURE. A mass-anisotropy trend among the classical dwarfs is a
  statement about the tracers and the modelling. Hayashi+2020 fit beta_z jointly with the halo axis ratio Q and
  the inclination and report the Q-beta_z degeneracy themselves; fainter dwarfs have fewer member velocities and
  their beta_z drifts toward whatever that degeneracy favours. No gravity theory is being discriminated.

  WHAT SURVIVES: nothing that g03 did not already concede. Its A6 (Newtonian mutation keeps 64 per cent), A7
  (below the shuffle's own 95th percentile), A8-A11 (the input variable is not measured) and A13 (the arms
  agree on this observable) all stand and are correct. This file adds that the ONE thing g03 counted in the
  claim's favour -- that mass does not explain it -- is the opposite of true.

RESULT: 6 checks, 4 FAIL -> ['V1 (THE RESIDUAL IS A MASS ORDERING) for the correlation to be about orbits, the residual must carry rank information that stellar mass does not already carry. Spearman(resid, -log M*) must be below 0.9', 'V2 (VARIANCE BUDGET) for the residual to be a dynamical statistic, the kinematic term 2 log(sigma) must contribute at least as much spread as the photometric term 0.5 log(M*)', 'V3 (THE MUTATION THAT DECIDES IT) a trajectory-dependent modification expresses itself through the KINEMATICS. So deleting the kinematics -- setting every velocity dispersion to the sample mean and leaving only M*, R_half and Galactocentric distance -- must DESTROY the correlation with anisotropy', 'V4 (CURRENCY) the confound check must be computed in the same currency as the headline statistic. A Spearman headline requires a Spearman partial. The two must agree to within the null sd']   rc=1
